              "code": "N_NEVER_COMPLETE",
              "guide": "reads",
              "mechanical": false,
              "message": "query poolProposals: the page over proposals[..] by read or declaration on proposals (pool, at, id) at schema.q:64:12 is never `complete` \u2014 the read rule `.author = viewer or exists pools[.pool, owner = viewer]` reads .author, which the scan does not pin, so the server cannot tell \"no more\" from \"more you may not read\"; `exists pools[.pool, owner = viewer]` is decided by the scan's pins, but its holders cannot read the row it probes, so it does not decide the page: `complete: false` for them too; it answers `complete: false` even when every row the caller may read is there \u2014 test.cases.jsonl: the default passes it (or write `\"expect\": \"capped\"`); a client shows the rows it has and treats `complete: false` as \"there may be more\", not an error; the complete form requires the checked immutable copy: on table proposals declare `pool: pools on delete restrict`, add `poolOwner: principal` and `immutable pool, poolOwner`; replace its read declaration with `read <- .author = viewer or .poolOwner = viewer`; replace its insert declaration with `insert <- (.author = viewer and exists members[.pool, viewer] and .status = 'pending') and exists pools[.pool, owner = .poolOwner]`; in every `insert proposals { \u2026 }` payload add `poolOwner: poolOwnerSource.owner` after inserting `poolOwnerSource = pools[pool] ! else NOT_FOUND` immediately before that insert (using the same reference value supplied in its payload), and backfill that field from pools.owner in existing rows and trusted seeds before adoption. The parent declares `immutable owner`, so this preserves the original readers; a mutable parent owner instead requires transactional copy propagation (check --verbose)",
              "rewrite": "on table proposals declare `pool: pools on delete restrict`, add `poolOwner: principal` and `immutable pool, poolOwner`; replace its read declaration with `read <- .author = viewer or .poolOwner = viewer`; replace its insert declaration with `insert <- (.author = viewer and exists members[.pool, viewer] and .status = 'pending') and exists pools[.pool, owner = .poolOwner]`; in every `insert proposals { \u2026 }` payload add `poolOwner: poolOwnerSource.owner` after inserting `poolOwnerSource = pools[pool] ! else NOT_FOUND` immediately before that insert (using the same reference value supplied in its payload), and backfill that field from pools.owner in existing rows and trusted seeds before adoption. The parent declares `immutable owner`, so this preserves the original readers; a mutable parent owner instead requires transactional copy propagation\n\nOtherwise: pin .author with where equalities as well as the current equality prefix, choosing the intended scope (this narrows the answer); the existing index `read or declaration on proposals (author, pool, at, id) at schema.q:64:12: author, pool, at, id` supports it; or read the page on the device. As code: the parent-owner witness is not readable to every owner, so discovery cannot certify absence; on table proposals declare `pool: pools on delete restrict`, add `poolOwner: principal` and `immutable pool, poolOwner`; replace its read declaration with `read <- .author = viewer or .poolOwner = viewer`; replace its insert declaration with `insert <- (.author = viewer and exists members[.pool, viewer] and .status = 'pending') and exists pools[.pool, owner = .poolOwner]`; in every `insert proposals { \u2026 }` payload add `poolOwner: poolOwnerSource.owner` after inserting `poolOwnerSource = pools[pool] ! else NOT_FOUND` immediately before that insert (using the same reference value supplied in its payload), and backfill that field from pools.owner in existing rows and trusted seeds before adoption